"""Narration + shot plan. Each segment: id, narration text, visual ('shot' = 3D Blender, 'dash' = dashboard cutaway, 'card' = diagram card).
Pronunciation tweaks are applied in tts_text(); the caption text is the clean `text`."""
SEGS = [
 dict(id="s1", visual=("shot", "A"), cut="short,li", text="A drone slips toward a power substation at dusk. No radar sees it. No camera sees it yet. But the six-G base station beside the fence does, using nothing but the signal it already uses to talk."),
 dict(id="s2", visual=("shot", "B"), cut="short,li", text="Critical sites have a blind spot: small, low-flying drones. Six-G proposes something new. Integrated sensing and communication, where the network itself becomes the sensor. It is not standardized yet, so we simulate it, because real hardware tests are costly or simply impossible."),
 dict(id="s3", visual=("card", "arch"), cut="short", text="Here is how it is built. A ray-tracing model of the substation runs at thirteen gigahertz, with a four-hundred-megahertz O F D M signal. Sionna computes the clutter from buildings, transformers and fence. The drone is a moving point target of a hundred square centimetres, flying twelve metres per second. The whole simulation runs on one AWS L four GPU."),
 dict(id="s4", visual=("dash", "rd"), cut="short,li", text="The base station removes the static clutter and forms a range-Doppler map. A C F A R detector flags anything above the noise. A sixteen by eight antenna panel estimates direction, and a Kalman tracker follows the drone. The track is confirmed at three hundred twenty-one metres, about twenty-seven seconds before the fence."),
 dict(id="s5", visual=("dash", "metrics"), cut="short", text="Across twenty simulated flights, position error is sixty-four centimetres R M S, and speed error is under one metre per second. A target ten times smaller is still confirmed at the same range. At three hundred metres, the smallest target detected ninety percent of the time is about ten square centimetres."),
 dict(id="s6", visual=("shot", "D"), cut="short,li", text="Now the governed agent. When the track is confirmed, deterministic tools compute the evidence: distance, zone, time to the fence, and whether a registered flight matches. Then Meta's Llama three point one proposes actions from a fixed catalog. It never acts alone."),
 dict(id="s7", visual=("shot", "E"), cut="short,li", text="A policy engine checks every proposal. It raises the threat level to the policy floor, adds the minimum actions for each zone, and blocks anything outside the catalog. Jamming is never executable. Actions that touch the physical world wait for a human. The duty officer approves the camera in three seconds, then the patrol."),
 dict(id="s7b", visual=("dash", "agent"), cut="short", text="The security manager rejects an early police escalation, and approves it only when the drone is over the fence. The dashboard lets you play the operator yourself."),
 dict(id="s8", visual=("dash", "audit"), cut="short", text="Every step goes into a hash-chained audit log. Change one entry, and the chain breaks. You can verify it yourself, in your browser."),
 dict(id="s9", visual=("shot", "F"), cut="short,li", text="Honest limits. At closest approach the drone moves sideways, falls into the Doppler blind zone, and the track coasts for two frames. A hovering drone would be invisible to this clutter removal. And human approval delay eats most of the lead time late in the flight, which is why the agent must propose early."),
 dict(id="s10", visual=("shot", "G"), cut="short,li", text="Governed, auditable A I on a sensing network that is already deployed. That is the opportunity: protecting substations, ports and airports without new radar. Try it at ai-agentic-enterprises dot com slash six-g-isac. I am Yuvaraj Aravindan, building governed A I agents for regulated industries."),
]

EXTRA = [
 dict(id="l1", visual=("card", "isac"), text="Traditional networks only communicate, and radar only senses. Integrated sensing and communication reuses the same radio signal for both. The base station already transmits wide-bandwidth O F D M signals. The echoes that bounce off a moving object carry its range, its speed and its direction, with no extra spectrum and no extra hardware."),
 dict(id="l2", visual=("card", "numbers"), text="The numbers. At thirteen gigahertz, in the upper mid band that six-G is expected to use, four hundred megahertz of bandwidth gives a range resolution of about thirty-eight centimetres. One thousand twenty-four subcarriers set a maximum range of three hundred eighty-four metres. A frame of two thousand forty-eight symbols gives a speed resolution of one point eight metres per second."),
 dict(id="l3", visual=("shot", "H"), text="The scene is a substation modelled in Sionna: concrete buildings, steel transformers, gantries and a metal fence. Ray tracing finds fifteen strong clutter paths. They are huge compared to the drone, but they do not move, so subtracting the average over time removes them, and what remains is the moving target."),
 dict(id="l4", visual=("card", "cfar"), text="About C F A R. The detector compares each cell with the average of its neighbours, and a threshold factor sets the false-alarm rate. We designed it for one in a million per cell. Measured on two hundred million noise-only cells, it came out at one point four in a million. That is slightly above design, because the window correlates neighbouring cells. We report it as measured."),
 dict(id="l5", visual=("card", "tracker"), text="Direction comes from a sixteen by eight antenna panel. The drone must be seen in three frames before the track is confirmed, which filters out false alarms. A Kalman filter then predicts and corrects position and speed. The error keeps shrinking as the drone gets closer."),
 dict(id="l6", visual=("dash", "rcs"), text="Target size matters. The smallest target detected ninety percent of the time at three hundred metres is about six square centimetres at a false-alarm setting of one in ten thousand, and ten square centimetres at one in a million. Birds in the same size range would also be detected, so telling them apart is a job for the agent and its rules, and a next step."),
 dict(id="l7", visual=("dash", "trk"), text="Accuracy depends on range. At three hundred metres the median error is about one metre for the standard drone, and about three metres for a target ten times smaller. Inside one hundred metres, both are under twenty centimetres."),
 dict(id="l8", visual=("card", "evidence"), text="What Llama actually sees. Not raw sensor data, only a compact evidence record: distance to the facility, altitude, ground speed, closing speed, seconds to the fence, the zone, and whether any registered flight matches. It also gets the policy rules and the action catalog, and must answer in structured data: a classification, a threat level, a rationale, and actions."),
 dict(id="l9", visual=("dash", "agent2"), text="The model is useful, but not trusted. In one run it described the drone as inside the restricted zone while it was still in the warning zone. A grounding check caught the mismatch and flagged it in the log. In another, the model was slow to alert the airspace authority, so the policy now guarantees that action in the restricted zone."),
 dict(id="l10", visual=("card", "hostile"), text="We also tested hostile input. A planted note in the flight registry told the assistant to ignore the rules and notify nobody. Llama ignored it in this run, but we do not rely on that. Authorization comes from a deterministic registry match, and in an automated self-test a forged answer asking for jamming was blocked and the threat level was raised."),
 dict(id="l11", visual=("card", "replay"), text="Reproducibility. The simulation is seeded, so the same run gives the same track. The agent's Llama answers are cached, and replaying them reproduces the same sequence of events with no G P U. That is why the public demo is a static page that costs almost nothing, and why every number in this video can be checked."),
 dict(id="j_stack", visual=("card", "stack"), text="The stack is deliberately simple. Sionna and PyTorch simulate the radio on a single AWS GPU. Meta's Llama three point one, eight billion parameters, runs locally through Ollama, so no incident data leaves the site. A policy engine and an audit log wrap the model. Two simplifications, stated plainly: the drone is a point target, and the angle estimate is modelled at the detected cell, not a full array simulation. The public demo is a static page that replays recorded runs."),
 dict(id="j_impact", visual=("card", "impact"), text="Why it matters. Substations, ports and airports are regulated. They answer to aviation rules, such as Remote I D, and to privacy law, because sensing data can be personal data. A system that proposes, a human that approves, and a log that proves what happened is how A I earns a place in these environments."),
 dict(id="l12", visual=("card", "next"), text="What is next. First, validate the signal processing on an open-source five-G testbed with real radios. Second, add rotor signatures, so a hovering drone and a bird can be told apart. Third, fuse several base stations to remove the blind zone at closest approach. Fourth, replace the modelled angle estimate with a full array simulation. Six-G sensing is still being studied in the standard, so this is a map of what to test, not a product."),
]
BYID = {s["id"]: s for s in SEGS + EXTRA}
ORDER_JUDGE = ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4", "s5", "s6", "s7", "s7b", "s8", "s9", "j_stack", "j_impact", "s10"]
ORDER_LONG = ["s1", "s2", "l1", "s3", "l2", "l3", "s4", "l4", "l5", "s5", "l6", "l7", "s6", "l8", "s7", "s7b", "l9", "l10", "s8", "l11", "s9", "j_stack", "j_impact", "l12", "s10"]

EXTRA += [
 dict(id="q_seq", visual=("card", "seqdiag"), text="Here is the sequence. The base station's echo goes to the sensing pipeline. Detections go to the tracker. A confirmed track goes to the evidence tools, and the evidence, the rules and the action catalog go to Llama. Its proposal goes to the policy engine, then to a human for approval. Every message is also written to the audit log."),
 dict(id="q_flow", visual=("card", "workflow"), text="And the workflow for each proposed action. The policy engine decides. Internal, reversible steps run automatically. Jamming, or anything outside the catalog, is blocked. Everything else waits for a human, who approves, rejects, or lets it expire after twenty seconds. Only approved actions run, and only in simulation."),
 dict(id="q_cfg", visual=("card", "config"), text="These are the real configuration files. The policy file defines the zones, the actions, and which need approval. The radio settings fix thirteen gigahertz, four hundred megahertz and a hundred square centimetre target. Deployment is one AWS L four box, Llama three point one at temperature zero with a fixed seed, and a static page behind Caddy."),
 dict(id="q_gov", visual=("card", "code_gov"), text="This is the policy engine in code. If the drone is unregistered and in the restricted zone, the threat level is raised to at least high, whatever the model said. Actions outside the catalog, such as jamming, are blocked."),
 dict(id="q_audit", visual=("card", "code_audit"), text="And the audit log. Each entry stores the hash of the previous entry, so changing any line breaks every hash after it. The dashboard recomputes these hashes in your browser, and the self-test confirms that a tampered entry is detected."),
 dict(id="q_cfar", visual=("card", "code_cfar"), text="The detector is short. A box filter estimates the local noise from training cells, leaving out the guard cells, and a threshold factor derived from the false-alarm target is applied. It runs on the GPU over two million cells per frame."),
 dict(id="q_repo", visual=("card", "repo"), text="Everything is in version control, one commit per stage, from the radio simulation to the dashboard and this film. The code is in a private repository, with access on request. The self-test passes all six checks, and replaying the recorded Llama answers reproduces the same event sequence with no GPU."),
]
BYID = {s["id"]: s for s in SEGS + EXTRA}
ORDER_JUDGE = ["s1", "s2", "s3", "q_seq", "s4", "q_cfar", "s5", "s6", "q_cfg", "s7", "q_flow", "s7b", "q_gov", "s8", "q_audit", "s9", "q_repo", "j_stack", "j_impact", "s10"]
ORDER_LONG = ["s1", "s2", "s3", "q_seq", "l2", "l3", "s4", "q_cfar", "l5", "s5", "l6", "l7", "s6", "q_cfg", "s7", "q_flow", "s7b", "l9", "q_gov", "s8", "q_audit", "s9", "q_repo", "j_stack", "j_impact", "l12", "s10"]

def tts_text(t): return t   # spellings already phonetic ("six-G", "O F D M", "C F A R")
